@@ -0,0 +1,84 @@
+# AsusSMC for X1605VA — 1.0.0
+
+The first release of the X1605VA fork is based on `hieplpvip/AsusSMC`
+commit `c15d2f297c86b65a3cb866cc2bcece595c136417`. Upstream MIT licensing
+and copyright notices are retained. The fork identifier is
+`com.samuelnotfound.AsusSMC`; the service class and filename remain
+`AsusSMC` and `AsusSMC.kext`.
+
+## Scope
+
+- Correct BIOS 305 WMI fan RPM decoding and continuous polling.
+- Build and sort SMC keys before VirtualSMC registration.
+- Standard `FNum`, `F0Ac`, and `F0ID` monitoring keys.
+- Root-only Auto/Full Speed and Standard/Whisper/Performance commands.
+- Cached thermal-profile readback from the Vivobook policy endpoint.
+- Percentage keyboard-backlight control through existing `GKPC`/`SKPC`
+  ACPI methods from `SSDT-KBLU` or the combined Assistant SSDT.
+- No new microphone, screen-brightness, trackpad, or hotkey-remapping feature.
+  Existing upstream AsusSMC behavior is retained.
+
+The UI is the separate [X1605VA Settings project](https://github.com/samuelnotfound/Asus-X1605VA-Hackintosh-Tahoe/tree/main/Projects/ASUS/X1605VASettings).
+
+## Versioning
+
+`VERSION` is the SemVer release version, starting at **1.0.0**. The canonical
+build script uses it for the bundle and module versions. Control API version
+1 is a compatibility number. `X1605VA-FanPatch = 1.0.0` is a retained legacy
+diagnostic, not a separately versioned product. The upstream commit identifies
+ancestry independently of fork release numbering.
+
+## Build
+
+```sh
+bash build-x1605va.sh
+```
+
+Requires Command Line Tools. Uses MacKernelSDK
+`3f750085caa17ec3a7880f11c11bf4f48cd6a164`, Lilu 1.7.2 and VirtualSMC 1.3.8
+SDK headers. Output: `build-x1605va/AsusSMC.kext`. Protocol tests and ad-hoc
+signature verification run during build. Nothing is installed or loaded.
+
+For a deliberate test boot, replace the existing AsusSMC bundle rather than
+adding another copy. Keep Lilu and VirtualSMC before it. `-x1605vafan` enables
+monitoring/backlight; `-x1605vafancontrol` additionally permits administrator
+fan commands. Keep the existing working keyboard SSDT. No new SSDT is added.
+Avoid overlapping fan/ALS keys from SMCSuperIO or SMCLightSensor in the test
+configuration. The build never changes EFI or boot arguments.
+
+## Interface
+
+All new firmware operations run on the sensor workloop. The property handler
+validates and queues one request; it never evaluates ACPI. User-space reads
+consume cached IORegistry properties.
+
+Fan requests contain exactly `FanCommand` and `FanRequestID` (nonzero uint32).
+They require administrator privilege, both boot arguments, and a ready
+workloop. Commands: 1 Auto, 2 Full Speed, 3 Standard, 4 Whisper, 5 Performance.
+Results use `FanCompletedRequestID`, `FanCommandReply`, and
+`FanCommandSucceeded`. Reply 1 is firmware acknowledgement. The older fanctl
+1.0.1 interface remains supported, including unsigned 32-bit request IDs.
+
+Keyboard requests contain exactly `KeyboardPercent` (0–100) and
+`KeyboardRequestID` (nonzero uint32). They require local console-user or
+administrator privilege and a discovered `GKPC`/`SKPC` provider. Results use
+`KeyboardCompletedRequestID`, `KeyboardCommandReply`, and
+`KeyboardCommandSucceeded`. No arbitrary EC offsets or method names can be
+submitted. Fan and keyboard writes share one queue.
+
+`ThermalProfile`: 0 Standard, 1 Whisper, 2 Performance.
+`ThermalProfileValid` indicates a valid firmware read. `KeyboardPercent` and
+`KeyboardReadingValid` describe the backlight cache, not measured illumination.
+`FanRequestedMode` is the last acknowledged request in this driver session,
+not independent firmware mode readback.
+
+No new setting is written at boot, login, or wake. No daemon is installed.
+A timeout cannot cancel an in-flight firmware call. Arbitrary RPM targets,
+calibrated minimum/maximum RPM and custom fan curves are not implemented.
+
+## Validation limits
+
+The preceding 1.4.2 driver verified live RPM and physical Auto/Full Speed on
+X1605VA BIOS 305; profile commands received successful firmware replies. The
+1.0.0 additions need a deliberate hardware test. Keyboard control, profile
+readback and sleep/wake are not established by compilation or fixture tests.
